\description{
Function(s) to compute prevalence statistics.
}
\section{Functions}{

\strong{popEpi::prev_lexis}

\code{popEpi::prev_lexis} can be used to compute numbers of (potentially
effective numbers of) subjects remaining in follow-up at arbitrary time
points. It performs the following steps:
\itemize{
\item For each observation time point in \code{observation_time_points[[1]]}:
\itemize{
\item Calls \verb{[lexis_split_merge_aggregate_by_stratum]} with the
\code{lexis}, \code{subset}, \code{aggre_by} supplied to
\code{popEpi::prev_lexis} and with
\code{aggre_exprs = list(n_prev = quote(.N))}, and \code{breaks = stratum_breaks}.
This produces a table stratified by
\code{aggre_by} and all time scales used in \code{stratum_breaks}.
The only value column at this point is \code{n_prev}, the number
of subjects in follow-up at the given observation time point.
\item If \code{!is.null(merge_dt)},
collect subjects in \code{lexis} who were censored before the
current observation time point. This is defined as those in
\code{lexis} who have \code{lexis[["lex.Cst"]] == lexis[["lex.Xst"]]} and
\code{lexis[[obs_ts_col_nm]] + lexis[["lex.dur"]] < obs_tp}, where
\code{obs_ts_col_nm = names(observation_time_points)} and
\code{obs_tp} is the current observation time point. These collected
subjects are the ones we need to extrapolate to the current
observation time point.
\item If there are no observations that need to be extrapolated
then we end the extrapolation step early and simply use
\code{n_prev_eff = n_prev}. Otherwise proceed as described below.
\item If \code{inherits(merge_dt, "list")}, collect arguments from
\code{merge_dt} to call \verb{[surv_lexis]}.
\item However, arguments \code{lexis}, \code{subset}, and \code{estimators = "S_ch"}
are assigned
internally to the respective arguments supplied to \code{prev_lexis}
and cannot be overridden.
\item Note that \code{lexis} is the input argument \code{lexis} and not only
those whose
follow-up we need to "extrapolate". So estimation of survival
is based on all subjects (in the \code{subset}).
\item We set as overrideable defaults
\itemize{
\item \code{aggre_by} as-is,
\item \code{breaks} as monthly intervals up to 5 years of follow-up
and thereafter as annual intervals. Note that this
default only works correctly if your time scales are
specified in fractional years so that e.g.
\code{lex.dur = 5.1} means 5.1 years of follow-up.
The last interval ends where the longest
"extrapolation" ends, e.g. for observation time point
2021.99 and the earliest extrapolatable observation
starting at \code{ts_cal = 1995.53} the last interval
is \verb{]26, 27]}. From \code{ts_cal = 2020.13} the last interval
is \verb{]1.83333, 1.91667]}.
\item \code{split_merge_aggregate_optional_args} contains element
\code{breaks_collapse_args} with value
\code{list(test_expr = quote(sum(lex.dur)))}.
Therefore by default we re-aggregate survival intervals
to avoid empty ones.
}
\item If \code{inherits(merge_dt, "list")}, we also interpolate survival
estimates in the table we have just produced so that there
are always 1000 intervals starting from zero and ending where
the last survival estimate is available. This is peformed to
ensure that the survival estimates we merge will be available
at a sufficient "resolution". For instance, even if the
survival estimates are for one-year intervals due to sparsity,
we get a reasonable survival estimate for a subject who was
censored at 1.01 --- from somewhere close to 1.01 instead from
2.0.
\item If you passed \code{breaks} via \code{merge_dt} with multiple time
scales (e.g. for period analysis), the time scales other
than the survival time one are removed from the table of
survival estimates if the time scale only defined one
interval. For instance if you used
\code{ts_cal = c(2020, 2022.9999)} then the table of survival
estimates contains only one period and we discard the
\code{ts_cal_start} and \code{ts_cal_stop} columns. This is to
enable computing e.g. period analysis survival and then
allowing those results to be applied to observations
outside of the period. Otherwise e.g. period analysis
survival estimates for \code{ts_cal = c(2020, 2022.9999)}
would not be merged to an extrapolateable observation at
\code{ts_cal = 2019.5}. But if multiple intervals are defined
then those are kept, e.g.
\code{ts_cal = c(2020, 2023, 2026)} leads to the period
estimates \code{2020-2022} and \code{2023-2025}. If you do period
analysis here, you must make sure to only use one period
or the ensure that the estimated multiple periods cover
all the extrapolateable observations.
\item Merge (for the first time) \code{merge_dt} with the collected
subjects at the original exit time of each
subject. This yields the survival probability for each
subject at exit, in math we name this \code{S(t_e)}.
E.g. subject was censored at \code{t_e = 2.1} means
\code{S(t_e) = S(2.1)}.
\item Merge \code{merge_dt} for the second time, this time at the
current prevalence observation time point such as at
\code{ts_cal = 2009.999}.
In math this is \code{S(t_p)} where \code{t_p} is the prevalence
observation time point. For instance for one subject \code{t_p}
might be \code{t_p = 5.7} because they were censored 5.7 years
before the prevalence observation time point. Then we use
\code{S(t_p) = S(5.7)}.
\item With both \code{S(t_e)} and \code{S(t_p)} available, our
"extrapolated" or
"effective" for one subject for being in follow-up is
between zero and
one for each subject and defined simply as the conditional
survival up to \code{t_p} starting from \code{t_e},
\code{S(t_p|t_e) = S(t_p) / S(t_e)}. E.g.
\code{S(t_p) / S(t_e) = S(5.7) / S(2.1) = 0.8 / 0.9 ~ 0.8888889}.
\item Call \verb{[lexis_split_merge_aggregate_by_stratum]} for the second
time, this time with the subjects collected for extrapolation,
and sum the number of extrapolated subjects in follow-up into a
table with the identical stratification as the one created
before.
\item Add column \code{n_prev_eff} as the sum of the number of extrapolated
subjects and the original \code{n_prev} into the first table we
created.
}
\item Collect the observation time point-specific results into one big table.
\item If supplied, merge data from \code{n_at_risk_dt} into the big table. To
accomplish this, any stratifying time scale columns defined via
\code{stratum_breaks} and found also in \code{n_at_risk_dt} (the rather usual one
being age, e.g. \code{ts_age}) is assumed to be a \code{factor} column with levels
that specify the lower and upper bound of each interval in brackets, e.g.
\verb{[0, 5[}, though the brackets are ignored. The lower and upper bounds are
made use of when merging the data from \code{n_at_risk_dt} into the big table,
as the latter contains the lower and upper bounds as separate columns.
E.g. column \code{ts_age} in \code{n_at_risk_dt} with levels
\code{c("[0,5[", "[5,10[", ..., "[80,85[", "[85,Inf[")} is turned into the two
columns \code{ts_age_start = c(0, 5, 10, ..., 80, 85)} and
\code{ts_age_stop = c(5, 10, 15, ..., 85, Inf)}. The big output table contains
the very same columns if one has supplied something like
\code{stratum_breaks = list(ts_age = c(0, 5, 10, ..., 80, 85, Inf), ...)}.
Then the merge is straightforward and the big table gains the additional
column \code{n_at_risk}.
\item If \code{weight_dt} was supplied, we harmonise it with the current big
output table. Similarly to \code{n_at_risk_dt}, it is possible to include
time scales from \code{stratum_breaks} in \code{weight_dt} as a factor with
levels which are bracketed intervals, e.g.
\code{c("[0,5[", "[5,10[", ..., "[80,85[", "[85,Inf[")}, following the
format produced by \verb{[cut]}. The lower and upper bounds are extracted
and matched with the correspoding columns in the current big output
table, e.g. with \code{ts_age_start} and \code{ts_age_stop}.
\item \verb{[directadjusting::directly_adjusted_estimates]} is called to produce
adjusted estimates of prevalence proportions if \code{weight_dt} was
supplied or non-adjusted ones of not. Both \code{p_prev} and \code{p_prev_eff}
are passed to \verb{[directadjusting::directly_adjusted_estimates]}, if
the latter was estimated.
\item Even if adjusting is performed, the output table will contain
"raw" sums of \code{n_at_risk}, \code{n_prev}, and \code{n_prev_eff} if it was
computed.
}
}

\examples{
# this data.table::setDTthreads call is included here only to
# conform to the CRAN submission requirement to only use at most 2
# threads. you do not need to set this to use popEpi.
# however some long calculations may benefit from using more threads.
data.table::setDTthreads(2L)

# popEpi::prev_lexis
make_sire <- function() {
  sire <- popEpi::sire
  sire <- sire[
    sire[["dg_date"]] < sire[["ex_date"]] &
      sire[["ex_date"]] >= as.Date("1999-01-01") &
      (get.yrs(sire[["ex_date"]]) - get.yrs(sire[["bi_date"]])) < 100
  ]
  # you can also use popEpi::Lexis_dt
  sire <- Epi::Lexis(
    data = sire,
    entry = list(
      ts_cal = popEpi::get.yrs(dg_date),
      ts_age = popEpi::get.yrs(dg_date) - popEpi::get.yrs(bi_date),
      ts_fut = 0.0
    ),
    duration = popEpi::get.yrs(ex_date) - popEpi::get.yrs(dg_date),
    entry.status = 0L,
    exit.status = status
  )
  return(sire[])
}
sire <- make_sire()

# observed counts only
pdt <- popEpi::prev_lexis(
  lexis = sire,
  aggre_by = "sex",
  observation_time_points = list(ts_cal = 2011.99),
  stratum_breaks = list(
    ts_age = c(seq(0, 85, 5), Inf),
    ts_fut = c(0, 1, 5, 10, Inf)
  )
)
stopifnot(
  inherits(pdt, "data.table"),
  "sex" \%in\% names(pdt),
  "n_prev" \%in\% names(pdt)
)

# + effective counts with survival table for computing expected counts
sdt <- popEpi::surv_lexis(
  lexis = sire,
  breaks = list(
    # it is best to have smaller intervals initially but they can be rather
    # wide after a while
    ts_fut = c(seq(0, 1, 1 / 12), seq(2, 50, 1))
  ),
  aggre_by = "sex",
  first_record_by = "lex.id",
  split_merge_aggregate_optional_args = list(
    # we allow for collapsing intervals to ensure none are totally empty
    breaks_collapse_args = list(
      test_expr = quote(sum(lex.dur))
    )
  )
)
# sdt goes ultimately to popEpi::lexis_merge, so we massage it a bit first
data.table::set(
  x = sdt,
  j = "ts_fut",
  value = data.table::fctr(paste0(
    "]",
    sdt[["ts_fut_start"]],
    ",",
    sdt[["ts_fut_stop"]],
    "]"
  ))
)
data.table::setnames(x = sdt, "S_ch", "S")
sdt <- data.table::setDT(as.list(sdt)[c(
  "sex",
  "ts_fut",
  "S"
)])
pdt <- popEpi::prev_lexis(
  lexis = sire,
  aggre_by = "sex",
  observation_time_points = list(ts_cal = 2011.99),
  stratum_breaks = list(
    ts_age = c(seq(0, 85, 5), Inf),
    ts_fut = c(0, 1, 5, 10, Inf)
  ),
  merge_dt = sdt
)
stopifnot(
  inherits(pdt, "data.table"),
  "sex" \%in\% names(pdt),
  c("n_prev", "n_prev_eff") \%in\% names(pdt),
  !is.na(pdt[["n_prev_eff"]])
)

# with survival estimated on the fly
surv_lexis_arg_list <- list(
  breaks = list(
    # optional period analysis estimation approach
    ts_cal = c(2001.99, 2011.99),
    # it is best to have smaller intervals initially but they can be rather
    # wide after a while
    ts_fut = c(seq(0, 1, 1 / 12), seq(2, 50, 1))
  ),
  split_merge_aggregate_optional_args = list(
    # we allow for collapsing intervals to ensure none are totally empty
    breaks_collapse_args = list(
      test_expr = quote(sum(lex.dur))
    )
  ),
  estimators = "S_ch"
)
pdt <- popEpi::prev_lexis(
  lexis = sire,
  aggre_by = "sex",
  observation_time_points = list(ts_cal = 2011.99),
  stratum_breaks = list(
    ts_age = c(seq(0, 85, 5), Inf),
    ts_fut = c(0, 1, 5, 10, Inf)
  ),
  merge_dt = surv_lexis_arg_list
)
stopifnot(
  inherits(pdt, "data.table"),
  "sex" \%in\% names(pdt),
  c("n_prev", "n_prev_eff") \%in\% names(pdt)
)

